code_review.final_verdict:
VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED

The change is correctly scoped and additive (DTO-only derived field, OpenAPI schema updated to match), but the way the formatting logic is expressed breaks the transform idiom this very file already establishes.

Finding — extract the display-name formatting into a default method instead of an inline `expression` string:

- `OwnerMapper` already has a clear local convention for derived/transformed values: real Java `default` methods (`normalizeTelephone`, `normalizeEmail`). The new `displayName` mapping instead embeds the logic as a stringly-typed MapStruct `expression = "java(owner.getLastName() + \", \" + owner.getFirstName())"`. That string is not compile-checked at authoring time, carries escaped quotes and manual concatenation, and is harder to unit-test than the adjacent helpers — a maintainer of this file would expect the same `default`-method treatment.

- Suggested shape, consistent with the existing helpers:
  ```java
  @Mapping(target = "displayName", expression = "java(toDisplayName(owner))")
  OwnerDto toOwnerDto(Owner owner);

  default String toDisplayName(Owner owner) {
      return owner.getLastName() + ", " + owner.getFirstName();
  }
  ```
  This keeps the formatting in plain, compiled Java alongside `normalizeTelephone`/`normalizeEmail`, matching the unit and style already used for per-field transforms here.

One thing worth a sentence of thought (not blocking): "LastName, FirstName" is arguably a domain/presentation concern that could live as `Owner.getDisplayName()`. Keeping it in the mapper is defensible since it's a DTO-only field, so I'd leave placement in the mapper — just as a proper method rather than an inline expression.

